Research Integrity Ledger
=========================

Context
-------

Proof of concept (PoC) in C++17 of an append-only, hash-chained ledger that
protects the integrity of research artifacts: datasets, scripts, results and
reports. The question it answers is: "Is this file exactly the one I registered,
and has the registration history not been rewritten?"

In research, it is common to need to demonstrate that:

* The dataset that was analyzed is the same one that was collected.
* The results were not altered after the analysis.
* An experiment is being re-run from exactly the same inputs.

Cases of scientific misconduct and the so-called "reproducibility crisis"
highlight the need for simple, verifiable mechanisms to support these claims.
This PoC demonstrates one of them.

How It Works
------------

A hash function (SHA-256) maps any input to a fixed-size output (256 bits). The
hash works as a "fingerprint" of the file. Relevant properties:

* Deterministic: the same input always produces the same hash.
* Avalanche effect: flipping 1 bit of the input changes ~50% of the hash bits.
* Preimage and collision resistant: it is computationally infeasible to find a
  different input that produces the same hash.

Each ledger entry stores the hash of the previous entry, and its own hash
covers all of its fields, including that previous hash:

Entry 0              Entry 1              Entry 2
┌─────────────────┐  ┌─────────────────┐  ┌─────────────────┐
| idx, timestamp  |  | idx, timestamp  |  | idx, timestamp  |
| file, sha256    |  | file, sha256    |  | file, sha256    |
| prev = 000...0  | >| prev = hash(E0) | >| prev = hash(E1) |
| hash = H(E0)    |/ | hash = H(E1)    |/ | hash = H(E2)    |
└─────────────────┘  └─────────────────┘  └─────────────────┘
entry_hash = SHA256( idx | timestamp | file | file_sha256 | prev_hash )

Editing, removing or reordering any old entry changes its hash, which breaks
the `prev` field of the next one, and so on to the end of the chain.

The `verify` command checks two independent things:

* Chain integrity: edited, removed or reordered ledger entries.
* File integrity: registered files that were modified or deleted afterwards.

Quick Start
-----------

No external dependencies: SHA-256 is implemented in the source itself
(FIPS 180-4).

* Build: g++ -std=c++17 -O2 -Wall -o integrity main.cpp

* ./integrity register data.csv   # computes the SHA-256 and appends an entry to ledger.log
* ./integrity verify              # checks chain and files (exit codes below)
* ./integrity list                # prints all entries

Exit codes: 0 = integrity confirmed, 1 = verification failed, 2 = usage or
input error (bad arguments, unreadable file, malformed ledger).

The ledger is created as `ledger.log` in the current directory. File paths are
stored exactly as given, so always run the tool from the same folder.

Ledger Format
-------------

Plain text, one entry per line, fields separated by `|`:

* idx|unix_timestamp|file|file_sha256|prev_hash|entry_hash

The first entry uses 64 zeros as its `prev_hash` (the genesis block). Since `|`
is the field separator, file names containing `|` are rejected.

Code Structure
--------------

* `Sha256`: SHA-256 implementation with streaming input.
* `sha256_str` / `sha256_file`: hashing of strings and files (files are read in
  64 KB chunks, without loading everything into memory).
* `Entry`: ledger entry structure and computation of its hash.
* `load`: reads and parses the ledger.
* `cmd_register` / `cmd_verify` / `cmd_list`: CLI commands.

The hashing and ledger primitives (`Sha256`, `Entry`, `load`) are independent of
the command-line interface. The `cmd_*` functions still print directly to the
console, so exposing this over an HTTP server in the future would first require
refactoring them to return results instead of printing.

Test Scenarios
--------------

All executed and validated:

* Hash of "abc" matches `sha256sum` (`ba7816bf...15ad`).
* Hash of a 300 KB binary file matches `sha256sum`.
* Nothing changed: `integrity confirmed`.
* A registered file was modified: `FILE MODIFIED`.
* A registered file was deleted: `FILE missing`.
* A field of a ledger entry was edited: `ENTRY tampered`.
* A middle ledger entry was removed: `INDEX mismatch` + `CHAIN broken`.
* The last ledger entry was removed: NOT detected (see Limitations).

Limitations and Threat Model
----------------------------

The PoC protects against accidental or naive changes, but not against an
adversary who controls the ledger:

* Full chain rewrite: anyone with write access to `ledger.log` can edit an entry
  and recompute all following hashes, since no secret is required. The defense
  is to anchor the chain head somewhere the attacker does not control (an e-mail
  to your advisor, a public repository, a Zenodo DOI, an RFC 3161 timestamping
  service, a public blockchain).
* No authenticity: there is no digital signature, so the ledger does not prove
  *who* registered a file.
* Untrusted timestamps: the time comes from the local clock, which can be
  changed.
* Tail truncation: removing the last entries is not detected unless the chain
  head was anchored externally.
* No concurrency control: two processes registering at the same time may corrupt
  the ledger.
* Custom SHA-256 is educational: in production, use an audited library (OpenSSL,
  libsodium).

Roadmap
-------

* Digital signatures (Ed25519): sign each entry for authenticity and
  non-repudiation.
* External anchoring: RFC 3161 timestamps or publication of the chain head.
* Merkle tree: verify a single file without walking the whole chain, with
  inclusion *proofs*.
* Metadata recording: software version, environment, experiment parameters.
* Server mode: HTTP API for multiple researchers, with authentication.
* Integration with scientific repositories: Zenodo, OSF, Git.
* Experimental evaluation: performance (time vs. dataset size), resistance to
  simulated attacks.

